Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy failed mainly because it waited for the model to decide
// when to open `<<`, so most decoding stayed unconstrained and rarely produced
// well-formed arithmetic spans. This revision makes delimiter production
// explicit and frequent: outside a constrained span, if the recent output does
// not already contain `<<`, the strategy strongly biases the next token toward
// `"<<"`; once inside, it keeps the span short and syntax-safe by using only
// verified constrained helpers until either the arithmetic fragment is complete
// or a small per-span token budget is reached, at which point it closes the
// span if possible.
//
// To improve syntax rate, the inside-span policy is simple and conservative.
// If the current constrained prefix is complete, it closes immediately. If the
// span grows too long, it rolls back to a valid arithmetic boundary and then
// either closes (if complete) or takes one adaptive constrained step. Normal
// inside-span growth uses AdaptiveConstrainedStep or GroupBoostedConstrainedStep,
// both of which guarantee parser-valid next tokens or EOS. This avoids the
// malformed partial expressions seen in evaluation.
//
// To ensure the output actually contains `<< >>`, the strategy tracks whether
// any opening delimiter has appeared in `generated`. Before the first span is
// opened, unconstrained decoding is replaced by a manual logits step that
// boosts `"<<"` very strongly. After at least one span has appeared, ordinary
// unconstrained stepping resumes, but any later sampled `"<<"` is still handled
// correctly by entering constrained mode. EOS is always terminal.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the forced-open/manual-unconstrained branch, if the
//   sampled token is not `"<<"`, insideConstrainedOut stays false, so the
//   invariant is vacuous; if it is `"<<"`, we set currentConstrainedOut := [],
//   which is valid by the precondition parser.IsValidPrefix([]). In the normal
//   unconstrained branch, the same reasoning applies. In the close branch,
//   CloseConstrainedSpan makes insideConstrainedOut false, so the implication is
//   vacuous. In the rollback branch, RollbackToBoundary returns a valid prefix;
//   if that repaired prefix is complete we close, otherwise we keep the repaired
//   prefix and then append one helper-sampled valid token, preserving validity.
//   In the adaptive/group-boosted append branch, the helper guarantees EOS or a
//   parser-valid next token, and AppendConstrainedToken preserves validity.
// suffix: When outside constrained mode, currentConstrainedOut == [], so the
//   suffix invariant is vacuous. Opening a span appends `"<<"` to generated and
//   resets currentConstrainedOut to [], so the length-0 suffix matches []. The
//   close branch resets currentConstrainedOut to [], making the implication
//   vacuous. In the rollback branch, repaired is a prefix of the old
//   currentConstrainedOut, and generated is trimmed by exactly the removed tail,
//   so generated's new trailing |repaired| tokens equal repaired; if one more
//   constrained token is appended, AppendConstrainedToken preserves the suffix
//   relation. In the adaptive/group-boosted append branch, the same token is
//   appended to generated and currentConstrainedOut, so the suffix relation is
//   preserved.
// cost accounting: cost is initialized to 0 and not changed until the final
