Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy failed because it tried to enter constrained mode almost
// immediately and then relied on the parser to grow a span token-by-token until
// completion. In evaluation, that produced very short malformed outputs like
// `<<n` and never reached a closing `>>`. The key fix is therefore behavioral,
// not local: stay unconstrained for normal explanatory text, and only open a
// constrained span when the model itself emits the opening delimiter token.
// Once inside a span, use a simple verified policy that either appends one
// parser-valid token, closes immediately when the constrained prefix is complete,
// or performs a bounded recovery by rolling back to a valid boundary and then
// spending one step. This directly targets the observed failures:
// - no forced early `<<`,
// - EOS remains terminal,
// - every complete constrained prefix is closed as soon as possible,
// - recovery cannot loop forever without consuming a step.
//
// To improve the chance of producing `<< >>` arithmetic snippets while keeping
// syntax valid, the unconstrained branch lightly boosts `"<<"` only after some
// ordinary text has already been generated and only if no constrained span has
// yet appeared. Inside constrained mode, the strategy uses adaptive constrained
// sampling over the caller-supplied token groups, then appends the sampled token
// with the verified append helper. If the constrained prefix becomes too long,
// it rolls back to a likely arithmetic boundary `"="`; if that does not change
// the state, it still consumes one step and breaks, preventing the pathological
// endless unterminated-span behavior seen in the traces.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In unconstrained branches, either EOS breaks or we remain
// outside constrained mode, so the implication is vacuous; if the sampled token
// is `"<<"`, OpenConstrainedSpan sets currentConstrainedOut to [], which is a
// valid parser prefix. In the close branch, insideConstrainedOut becomes false,
// so the implication is vacuous. In the append branch, AdaptiveConstrainedStep
// guarantees either EOS or a token whose append is parser-valid, and
// AppendConstrainedToken preserves that validity. In the rollback branch,
// RollbackToBoundary returns a parser-valid repaired prefix; if we then close,
// the implication is vacuous, and if we break after a no-op recovery, the loop
// ends without needing another preservation step.
// suffix: Outside constrained mode the implication is vacuous; opening a span
// with OpenConstrainedSpan yields currentConstrainedOut == [], so the empty
// suffix relation holds. In the close branch, currentConstrainedOut becomes [],
// making the implication vacuous. In the append branch, the same token is added
// to generated and currentConstrainedOut by AppendConstrainedToken, preserving
// the suffix equality. In the rollback branch, generated is trimmed by exactly
// the number of removed constrained tokens, so its trailing repaired-length
// suffix equals the repaired prefix.
// cost accounting: cost is initialized to 0 and returned as `steps`. Every
// non-breaking branch increments steps by exactly 1: UnconstrainedStep and
// OpenConstrainedSpan each cost one; the delayed-open manual branch uses
// ChooseNextTokenUnconstrained and explicitly bumps helpers.cost, then does
// `steps := steps + 1`; CloseConstrainedSpan and AdaptiveConstrainedStep each
// cost one and are followed by `steps := steps + 1`; the pure rollback recovery
// branch also does `steps := steps + 1` before breaking. Thus `cost := steps`
// satisfies `cost <= maxSteps`.
// progress: In unconstrained branches, generated grows by at most one token and
// steps increases by 1, so `|generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps` is
// preserved. Opening a span appends exactly one token (`"<<"`) and increments
// steps by 1. Closing appends at most one token and increments steps by 1.
// Adaptive constrained append adds exactly one token and increments steps by 1.
// Rollback may shrink generated; the no-op recovery branch still increments
// steps by 1 before breaking, so the bound remains true. Because every
// non-breaking branch strictly increases steps, the decreases clause is
// respected.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
